A||||4||Screwie||Torque question: lbs-ft vs. Nm||||1||
Z||000000||Screwie||11-13-2001||10:58 AM||||Does anybody know the conversion factor between lbs-ft versus the European standard for torque which is Nm (Newtonmeter)? Is it exactly 1:1?||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||11-13-2001||11:04 AM||||I don't have one handy but look in weights and measurements conversions of the New American Collegiate Dictionary.||||reg||14
Z||000002||JohnF||11-13-2001||11:12 AM||||Go here: <A HREF="http://www.ex.ac.uk/cimt/dictunit/cctorq.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ex.ac.uk/cimt/dictunit/cctorq.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-13-2001||11:36 AM||||Lets see: 1 pound force = 4.448 Newtons<BR>and 1 ft = 0.3048 meters so:<P>1/4.448 x 1/0.3048 = 1.355 ergo<P>1 pound ft = 1.355 Nm<P>The "Handbook of Chemsistry and Physics" gave the conversion as 1.35582, they must have used more decimal places.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Screwie||11-13-2001||11:46 AM||||Ah cool! Thanks guys!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
